# Manuscript PDF page 225

                              THE CHORD



   The stones had made the journey. My grandmother had stayed
home.
   I felt the current work its way through the knot my grip had be‐
come.
   And in a kitchen on the fourth floor, over a chair with a folded Con
Ed bill under one leg, my mother had her radio on.
   I don’t know how I knew. A merengue, full brass, a singer who did
not believe in subtlety, turned all the way up, rattling the seven glasses
on the shelf and the eighth beside them. She stood at the stove with a
dishtowel on her shoulder and her back to the room, and she did not
turn it down.
   The chord didn’t get louder.
   It grew wide enough that I could no longer hold all of it in my head,
however hard I pretended.
   At 10:07, Adrian Vale stood at the lectern with his black notebook
open and his pen on the page, and I saw him with nothing to write.
The pages he had filled that night had gone pale, the ink the color of a
receipt left in the sun. A line of gray text on the screen above his head
said MOMENT OF CALM, and the number under it, which had
climbed all night, was going down. His eyes went to the screen, then to
the stage, then to the hall. His mouth opened. He held the pen above
the paper for eleven minutes, and wrote nothing at all.
   Once, in those eleven minutes, he looked up from the page and
found me on the platform.
   “Neighbor,” he said into the microphone, and left the pause after it,
the one he had built for nervous people to fill.
   I didn’t fill it. Doña Fela did, from the third row, loud enough to
reach the roof: “¡Ese hombre no ha dicho nada en toda la noche!” That
man hasn’t said a thing all night. Somebody laughed. Somebody told
her to hush, and somebody else told him to hush, and the pause went
under a hundred arguments and didn’t come back up.
   Vale closed the notebook. It was the only sound he made.




